              "level": 0,
              "text": "Amyloidosis—no known therapy, unless the lesion is solitary and can be surgically removed.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Spherulocytosis—only effective treatment is surgical removal.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Idiopathic sterile granuloma and pyogranuloma:",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Prednisolone (2.2–4.4 mg/kg divided",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "PO q12h) is the first line of therapy; continue steroids for 7–14 days after complete remission, then taper dose.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Azathioprine (2.2 mg/kg q48h) or",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cyclosporine (modified, 5–10 mg/kg q24h) added as a steroid-sparing drug.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Foreign body reactions—removal of the offending substance; secondary deep bacterial infections require treatment with both topical and systemic antibiotics.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Canine eosinophilic granuloma—prednisolone (1.1–2.2 mg/kg PO q24h) usually effective.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cutaneous/systemic or malignant histiocytosis—no effective long-term therapy; see Histiocytosis, Cutaneous.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Calcinosis cutis—underlying disease must be controlled if possible; most cases require antibiotics to control secondary bacterial infections; hydrotherapy and frequent bathing in antibacterial shampoos minimize secondary infection; topical dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) is useful (applied to no more than one-third of the body once daily until lesions resolve); if lesions are extensive, serum calcium levels should be monitored closely while using DMSO.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Calcinosis circumscripta—surgical excision.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sterile nodular panniculitis:",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Single lesions can be removed surgically.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Prednisolone (2.2–4.4 mg/kg PO q24h or",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "divided PO q12h) treatment of choice; administer until lesions regress, then taper; some dogs remain in long-term remission, others require prolonged alternate-day therapy.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Azathioprine (2.2 mg/kg q48h) can be",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "used together with prednisolone as a corticosteroid-sparing agent.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Cyclosporine, modified (5–10 mg/kg",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "24h) can be used together with prednisolone or as an alternative to glucocorticosteroid therapy if the patient is steroid intolerant or steroids are not sufficiently effective.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Tetracycline (250 mg<10 kg, 500 mg",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": ">10 kg q8–12h), doxycycline (10 mg/kg",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "q24h), or minocycline (5 mg/kg q12h)",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": ">10 kg q8–12h), doxycycline (10 mg/kg"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "with niacinamide (250 mg<10 kg, 500 mg",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": ">10 kg q8–12h) can also be used as an",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "alternative to glucocorticoid therapy; this is",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": ">10 kg q8–12h) can also be used as an"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "effective only in mild cases.",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": ">10 kg q8–12h) can also be used as an"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Collagenous nevi—no therapy for most",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": ">10 kg q8–12h) can also be used as an"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cases since cystadenocarcinomas are usually",
              "pdf_page": 465,
              "printed_page": 383,
              "column": 2,
              "subheading": ">10 kg q8–12h) can also be used as an"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
